Jira Link: [DB-16545](https://yugabyte.atlassian.net/browse/DB-16545)
### Description

Currently this code does not save the DocDB next column ID as part of the snapshot so it is forced to estimate what it should be after the restore.  It does this by just taking one beyond the maximum DocDB next column ID in the snapshot schema.

Here is an example:

# What happens to column IDs when we backup and restore a database?

Suppose we backup a database with:
TABLE my_table:
  * column x, PG column ID 1, DocDB column ID 0
  * column y, PG column ID 2, DocDB column ID 1, marked deleted
  * column z, PG column ID 3, DocDB column ID 3
  * column q, PG column ID 4, DocDB column ID 4, marked deleted
  * next DocDB column ID: 5

Note that there is a skip in the DocDB column ID from 1 to 3 due to a failed ADD COLUMN DDL.

After restoring this ends up with:
TABLE my_table:
  * column x, PG column ID 1, DocDB column ID 0
  * column z, PG column ID 2, DocDB column ID 3
  * next DocDB column ID: 4

Notice:
  * Postgres column IDs can differ on the two sides
  * the DocDB column IDs for columns are the same on the two sides
  * the next DocDB column ID can differ between the two sides

(The above is from my understanding of the code rather than an actual test.)

The problem here is that the next DocDB column ID is 4 on the restored database but 5 on the database being backed up.

It may not be obvious, but this can cause major problems.  In particular, this allows reuse of DocDB column IDs with different types.  The following sample test:
```
~/code/yugabyte-db/src/yb/integration-tests/xcluster/xcluster_ddl_replication-test.cc:1840:
TEST_F(XClusterDDLReplicationTest, TBD) {
  if (!UseYbController()) {
    GTEST_SKIP() << "This test does not work with yb_backup.py";
  }
  ASSERT_OK(SetUpClusters(/*is_colocated=*/false, /*start_yb_controller_servers=*/true));

  {
    auto conn = ASSERT_RESULT(producer_cluster_.ConnectToDB(namespace_name));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("CREATE TABLE my_table (x INT);"));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("ALTER TABLE my_table ADD COLUMN y INT;"));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("ALTER TABLE my_table ADD COLUMN z INT;"));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("INSERT INTO my_table (x, y, z) VALUES (1,2,3), (4,5,6);"));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("ALTER TABLE my_table DROP COLUMN z;"));
  }

  ASSERT_OK(BackupFromProducer());
  ASSERT_OK(RestoreToConsumer());

  {
    auto conn = ASSERT_RESULT(consumer_cluster_.ConnectToDB(namespace_name));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("ALTER TABLE my_table ADD COLUMN q TEXT;"));
    ASSERT_OK(conn.Execute("INSERT INTO my_table (x, y, q) VALUES (7,8,'foobar');"));
  }

  auto GetRows = [&](Cluster& cluster) -> Result<std::string> {
    auto conn = VERIFY_RESULT(cluster.ConnectToDB(namespace_name));
    return conn.FetchAllAsString("SELECT * FROM my_table ORDER BY x", ", ", "\n");
  };

  auto producer_rows = ASSERT_RESULT(GetRows(producer_cluster_));
  auto consumer_rows = ASSERT_RESULT(GetRows(consumer_cluster_));
  ASSERT_EQ(producer_rows, consumer_rows);
}
```
demonstrates this by failing with:
```
2025-05-05 15:27:11.364 PDT [27332] ERROR:  Unexpected value type: kInt32
2025-05-05 15:27:11.364 PDT [27332] STATEMENT:  SELECT * FROM my_table ORDER BY x
/home/mdbridge/code/yugabyte-db/src/yb/integration-tests/xcluster/xcluster_ddl_replication-test.cc:1870: Failure
Failed
Bad status: Network error (yb/yql/pgwrapper/libpq_utils.cc:475): Fetch 'SELECT * FROM my_table ORDER BY x' failed: 7, message: ERROR:  Unexpected value type: kInt32 (pgsql error XX000) (aux msg ERROR:  Unexpected value type: kInt32)
```

This task is to save the DocDB next column ID as part of the snapshot then use it (if available) to restore this properly when the snapshot is imported as part of the restoration.

Unclear if legacy snapshots should be handled by scanning all the schemas or packing schemas and finding the maximum DocDB column ID in them.

(To be clear this discussion has talked about one table; there is one DocDB next column ID per table (really colocated table) and any given snapshot can have many tables.)


